      "Widely stocked at home centers",
      "Low upfront price per gallon",
      "Available in gloss and satin sheens",
      "Easy roller or sprayer application",
      "Adds mild color enhancement"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Shorter service life than premium acrylics",
      "Film needs recoating more often on exposed slabs",
      "Not suited to driveways with heavy salt use"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A reasonable choice for small walkways, steps and covered patios when budget and availability matter most.",
    "description": "Not every job justifies a specialty order. Seal-Krete Clear-Seal is the acrylic you can pick up on a Saturday, roll onto a front step or covered porch slab, and finish before dinner. It forms a light film that adds a mild sheen and some color depth, and it gives decent short-term resistance to water spotting and dirt. Its limits are the limits of budget acrylics in general. The film is thinner than the premium solvent acrylics, so it dulls and wears faster in sun and traffic, and owners commonly report recoating on a two-year cycle rather than three or four. On a driveway that sees road salt and tire turning, it is the wrong tool; a penetrating repellent will outlast it several times over. Where it makes sense is a small, sheltered surface where you want a light gloss, have a modest budget, and do not mind redoing it now and then."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Concrete Sealer",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-concrete-sealer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best all-around concrete sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For exterior concrete that should keep its natural look, a water-based silane-siloxane penetrating sealer such as Foundation Armor SX5000 is the most forgiving choice. It does not peel, yellow or change the texture, and it suits driveways, walks and patios alike. It is not the right pick for a garage floor that needs oil resistance or for anyone who wants a glossy finish."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use the same sealer on my driveway and garage floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but it will underperform on one of them. A penetrating repellent is ideal for the driveway and weak against oil in the garage. An epoxy coating is ideal for the garage and a poor choice outdoors, where sunlight yellows it and freeze-thaw can lift it. Most homes are best served by two products."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should I wait to seal new concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most film-forming sealers specify about 28 days so the slab can release moisture and gain strength. Some penetrating sealers and cure-and-seal products allow earlier application, but only follow that if the specific label says so. For a slab poured in fall, prioritize getting a penetrating sealer on before the first freeze and salt."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will sealing concrete stop it from cracking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Cracks come from shrinkage, settling, tree roots, heavy loads and freeze-thaw movement in the base. A sealer reduces surface damage such as scaling and spalling by keeping water out of the top layer, and that can slow the widening of small cracks. Fill existing cracks with a flexible sealant before sealing the slab."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a wet-look sealer bad for concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not inherently. A solvent or water-based acrylic protects the surface and enriches color. The drawbacks are that it wears and needs recoating every few years, can whiten if applied to damp concrete, and gets slippery when wet unless you add grit. On salted driveways a penetrating sealer is usually the better long-term choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I seal concrete in cold weather?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most products need air and surface temperatures above about 50 degrees Fahrenheit during application and for several hours after, with no frost overnight. Some penetrating sealers allow application down to around 40 degrees. Cold slows curing and can leave acrylics soft or cloudy. If the forecast is borderline, wait for a warmer, dry stretch."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know when it is time to reseal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Do a water drop test on the busiest part of the slab. If water soaks in and darkens the concrete within a minute or two, the protection is worn. On acrylic-sealed concrete, dull traffic lanes, flaking edges and water that no longer beads are signs. Reseal based on condition rather than a fixed number of years."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
