              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people shop for concrete sealer as if it were one product. It is closer to five. A sealer that is perfect on a salted driveway will do almost nothing for a garage floor that sees oil drips, and the glossy finish that makes a patio look new will peel off a damp basement floor within a year. Start with the slab, then pick the chemistry. The six picks below are sorted that way.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="match-the-slab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Slab, Not the Can
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fastest way to choose well is to name the threat each slab faces. A driveway in Ohio is fighting water, road salt and freeze-thaw cycles. A backyard patio in Arizona is fighting sun, pool chemicals and the look of chalky gray concrete. A garage floor is fighting oil and hot tires. A basement floor is fighting moisture that rises from below. Those four threats point to four different sealer families, and buying one product for all of them almost always shortchanges at least two.
          </p>

          <p>
            For driveways, sidewalks and steps that see winter, a penetrating silane-siloxane repellent is the default. It leaves nothing on the surface for shovels to scrape and keeps salty meltwater from soaking in and expanding when it freezes. Our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a> go deeper on that one job, including how densifiers and repellents can be paired on older, dusty slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For patios, pool surrounds and decorative concrete, the question is mostly appearance. If you like the slab as it looks dry, a penetrating sealer protects it invisibly. If you want richer color and a damp look, an acrylic film is the only way to get it, and you accept recoating every few years in exchange. Stamped and stained patios are a special case with their own rules, covered in our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">stamped concrete sealer guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage floors want a coating, not a repellent. Oil, antifreeze and brake fluid are not stopped well by water-repellent sealers, and the most common garage complaint is a permanent drip stain under the engine. A two-part epoxy or a polyaspartic system is what keeps those fluids on top. Basements want the opposite of a coating: a reactive silicate that tightens the concrete from inside without trapping vapor underneath.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Slab</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main threat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer family</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pick above</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
