        <div id="sealer-families" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Five Sealer Families, in Plain Terms
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Silane and siloxane repellents</strong> are small silicon-based molecules carried in water or solvent. They soak into the pores, react with moisture and the alkaline concrete, and leave the pore walls water-repellent. Nothing forms on the surface. Silane molecules are the smallest and reach deepest into dense concrete, siloxanes are larger and suit more porous masonry, and most retail products blend the two. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs. siloxane comparison</a> explains when the balance matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Silicate densifiers</strong>, sold as sodium, potassium or lithium silicate, react with the free lime in concrete to form extra binder inside the surface. They harden the top layer, reduce dusting and tighten the pores, but on their own they repel water only modestly. They are common on interior floors and on old, dusty exterior slabs as a first step before a repellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acrylic sealers</strong> are film formers. Solvent-based acrylics penetrate a little and give the strongest color enhancement; water-based acrylics are lower odor and usually a little less glossy. Either way, the film is what you see and what wears. Expect roughly two to four years outdoors before a recoat, depending on sun and traffic.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Polyurethanes</strong> are tougher films, often used as a topcoat over acrylic or epoxy where abrasion and chemical resistance matter. <strong>Epoxies</strong> are the hardest and most chemical-resistant of the common options, which is why they own the garage floor, but they yellow in sunlight and can fail on slabs with moisture coming through. Neither is a sensible pick for a typical driveway.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The One-Line Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Penetrating sealers protect the concrete; film sealers protect the look.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your goal is a slab that lasts longer and looks unchanged, buy a penetrating product. If your goal is a slab that looks richer, buy a film and budget for recoats. Trying to get both from one product is where most disappointment starts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="does-it-need-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does Your Concrete Need Sealing at All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every slab benefits equally. The quickest check is a water drop test. Sprinkle a cup of water on a clean, dry area and watch it for five minutes. If the concrete darkens quickly and the water disappears, the surface is absorbent and a sealer will make a real difference. If the water beads and sits on top, an old sealer is still working or the surface is dense enough that a penetrating product will struggle to get in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate matters more than age. Exterior concrete in regions with repeated freeze-thaw cycles and road salt gets the most benefit, because the damage mechanism there is water entering the surface, freezing, expanding and popping off thin flakes. In mild, dry regions, an unsealed broom-finished patio can go decades with only cosmetic staining. Sealing it still keeps it cleaner, but the payoff is smaller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some slabs should be left alone or treated with something else. Concrete with active structural cracks, heaving or settling needs repair first; sealer will not hold it together. A slab that already has a working film sealer should not get a penetrating product on top, because the repellent cannot reach the concrete through the film. And a slab with a moisture problem from below, such as a basement floor that sweats, should never get a non-breathable coating without first addressing the moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check what the concrete will be used for next. If you plan to paint, tile, epoxy or install flooring over it later, a silicone-based repellent can interfere with adhesion. Silicate densifiers usually do not, and many coating makers specifically allow them. Read the next product's data sheet before you seal something you plan to cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-or-old" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. New Concrete, Old Concrete and Previously Sealed Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            New concrete needs time. Most film-forming sealers specify waiting about 28 days so excess moisture can escape and the surface can gain strength. Sealing a green slab with an acrylic traps that moisture and is a common cause of a cloudy, white finish. Some penetrating silane-siloxane products and cure-and-seal products are labeled for earlier application, sometimes within days, but only if the label says so; do not assume it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first winter is the riskiest time for a new driveway or walk. The surface has not reached full strength, and a single season of salt can cause scaling that never goes away. If a slab is poured in late summer or fall, getting a penetrating repellent on before the first snow is worth more than any later treatment. Avoid deicers on concrete less than a year old where you can, and see our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt guide</a> for gentler options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Old concrete is mostly a cleaning job. Years of dirt, mildew, tire marks and oil block a sealer from soaking in. Pressure washing with a surface cleaner and a concrete degreaser on the stains is usually enough for bare concrete. Anything that will not lift, such as a deep oil spot, should be treated before sealing; once sealed over, it is locked in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Previously sealed concrete has one rule: match the chemistry or remove the old coat. A solvent acrylic can go over an old solvent acrylic. A water-based acrylic can usually go over a water-based acrylic after cleaning. Switching from a film to a penetrating sealer means stripping the film completely. Switching from a penetrating sealer to a film is usually possible once the repellent has weathered enough that water soaks in again; do a water drop test first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finish-and-slip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Finish, Sheen and Slip Resistance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sheen is not just cosmetic. Every film-forming sealer adds some smoothness to the surface, and that smoothness becomes slipperiness when the slab is wet, frosty or covered in pool water. High-gloss wet-look products are the worst offenders. Penetrating sealers leave the surface texture unchanged, so a broom-finished slab stays as grippy as it was before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you choose an acrylic for appearance on a walking surface, plan for traction. The standard fix is an anti-slip additive, a fine polymer grit that you stir into the final coat. It does not change the look much, but it does change the feel underfoot, especially on pool decks, steps and sloped walkways. Several acrylic makers sell their own additive; any fine grit made for coatings will work if you keep it stirred.
          </p>

          <p>
            Color enhancement follows the same scale as gloss. Solvent acrylics darken and enrich the most, water-based acrylics somewhat less, and penetrating products barely at all. Some penetrating sealers are sold in an enhancing version that darkens the concrete slightly without a film, which is a good middle path for stained or tinted slabs that look washed out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test before you commit. Seal a small, out-of-the-way patch and let it dry for a day. Check the color in sunlight, walk on it barefoot and in shoes, and wet it with a hose. That ten-minute check prevents the most common regret, which is a patio that looks great in photos and feels like ice after rain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-and-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Coverage Rates and What a Job Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage depends on how porous the concrete is, so labels give a range. A typical broom-finished exterior slab takes penetrating sealer at roughly 100 to 200 square feet per gallon per coat. Dense, trowel-finished garage floors soak up less, and old, weathered or porous concrete soaks up more. Acrylic sealers usually list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon for the first coat, with the second coat going further because the first has filled the pores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the area and add 10 to 15 percent for waste and thirsty spots. A two-car driveway of 20 by 40 feet is 800 square feet. At 150 square feet per gallon, two coats need roughly 11 gallons; a 5-gallon pail plus a few extra gallons, or two pails with leftover for the walk. Penetrating products often go on wet-on-wet, which uses a bit more than two separate coats would.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges at the Time of Writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0.15-$0.60</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Material cost per sq ft, DIY penetrating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100-200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, penetrating, per coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-4 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical exterior acrylic recoat cycle</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-10 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common penetrating service-life claims</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Hiring out costs more than the material, often several times more. Contractor pricing for sealing a residential slab varies widely by region and by whether cleaning and crack repair are included. Ask for a written quote that names the product, the number of coats and the coverage rate; a bid that does not name the product is a bid you cannot compare.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per year is the better comparison. A premium penetrating sealer that lasts seven or more years can be cheaper over a decade than a budget acrylic that needs redoing every two years, even if the pail costs twice as much. The exception is a small area where your time, not material cost, dominates; there a quick acrylic recoat can still make sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application-overview" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Application Overview: What Every Product Has in Common
          </h2>

          <p>
            The steps differ by product, but the skeleton is the same. Clean the slab thoroughly, repair cracks and spalls, let it dry completely, check the weather window, then apply thin, even coats. Most failures trace back to skipping one of those, not to the sealer itself. Our step-by-step <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">guide to sealing a concrete driveway</a> walks through each stage in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dryness is the step people rush. After pressure washing, give exterior concrete at least 24 hours of dry weather, longer in shade or cool temperatures. A simple check is to tape a square of plastic sheet to the slab for a few hours; if the underside fogs or the concrete darkens, it is still wet. Sealing damp concrete is the leading cause of a white, milky finish on acrylics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather sets the rest. Most labels call for surface and air temperatures roughly between 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, no rain for at least 12 to 24 hours, and no direct midday sun on a hot slab for film formers, which can bubble if the solvent flashes off too fast. Early morning or late afternoon in mild weather is ideal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tools follow the chemistry. Penetrating sealers go on best with a low-pressure pump sprayer, back-brushed or back-rolled to work out puddles. Acrylics go on with a sprayer or a roller with a short nap, in thin coats; thick coats trap solvent and turn cloudy. Solvent-based products need a sprayer with seals rated for solvents, or they will swell and leak within minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reseal-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long Each Type Lasts and When to Reseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Service life claims are best read as ranges for typical conditions. Silane-siloxane repellents are commonly rated for five to ten years on exterior concrete, with the low end on heavily salted, high-traffic driveways. Silicate densifiers are reactive and do not wear off the way a film does, though their water-repelling contribution is modest. Acrylics usually need refreshing every two to four years outdoors, sooner in full sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not reseal on a calendar alone. The water drop test tells you more than the date. When water starts soaking in and darkening the slab within a minute or two on a penetrating-sealed surface, it is time. On an acrylic, look for dull, worn traffic lanes, flaking at edges and water no longer beading in the most exposed areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recoating an acrylic is easier than the first coat, provided you stay with the same chemistry. Clean the surface, let it dry, and apply one thin coat. Two thick coats every year is a common way to build up a heavy film that eventually clouds, peels or becomes slippery. Less, applied less often, works better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a simple record: product name, date and coverage used. When it is time to reseal, you will know exactly what is on the slab and whether the next product is compatible. That single note prevents the most expensive mistake on this list, which is stripping a slab because nobody remembers what was on it.
          </p>
        </div>

