        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best concrete sealer is the one that matches the slab in front of you. For exterior concrete that should look the same and last longer, buy a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer; for heavily salted drives, step up to a concentrate built around chloride resistance. For a patio you want to look richer, choose a solvent acrylic and plan on recoats. Garage floors need an epoxy-type coating, and damp basements need a reactive silicate.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, the job is mostly preparation: clean, repair, dry, then thin coats in mild weather. Write down what you used. In five years, that note will save you a weekend of guessing, testing and possibly stripping.
          </p>
